
21 Mar 2023

There are dataset tests in the "output" directory. At the moment
we have four possible datasets that have been run on a variety of
machines. Different OS types and different CPU types have been used
to run these tests and we generally see consistent results.

There are problems with some systems where the fused multiply add
support seems to not exist. A fine example would be the VIA Eden
processor which will throw a SIGILL if the code is compiled with
the fma opcode support. Remove the fma option from the compiler
and then we get different numerical results. Welcome to the sad
world of floating point where sometimes the answer is "maybe".

Also the speed of computation for any of the test cases will vary
wildly. A Xeon may be orders of magnitude faster than the RISC-V
hardware produced by SiFive. In fact, the RISC-V hardware tested
is consistently the slowest ever seen and that includes running
on a 400MHz Pentium II machine from 1998.

